1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
 Polyvinyl chloride, PVC is the third on our list of engineering thermoplastics that we're going to talk about,

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:12,240
 and it's also the third most commonly produced thermoplastic in the world, accounting for around 10% of global plastic production.

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:18,160
 We may PVC these days in a range of different flavours as a hard stiff polymer for pipework, for example,

4
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,600
 and as a soft stretchy one for upholstery and clothing.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:28,040
 We polymerize PVC from the vinyl chloride monomer, which also looks an awful lot like the ethylene monomer.

6
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:33,760
 Like how in polypropylene, we only substituted one in every 400 in fingers to change the monomer.

7
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:39,360
 We do the same thing here with vinyl chloride, replacing one in every 400 with a chlorine atom.

8
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,840
 Pure PVC is a reasonably cheap and stiff thermoplastic that we often use in pipework.

9
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:52,880
 It's quite well suited to this application because it's reasonably resistant to acids and grimes and other nasties that you can imagine you'd find in your pipework.

10
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:59,600
 But pure PVC is not particularly UV or heat tolerant, meaning that it's not often used outdoors or to carry hot water, for example.

11
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:06,720
 There is a variant called CPVC, chlorinated PVC that has a little bit more chlorine in it, and generally is a little bit more heat tolerant.

12
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:12,880
 But, broadly speaking, if you're after a thermoplastic which is tolerant at high temperatures, you're probably not after PVC.

13
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:22,440
 By introducing additives called plasticizers in the manufacturer of PVC, we can essentially weaken the vander walls bonds of them to the point where they become very, very flexible.

14
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:30,320
 Confusingly, we also sometimes call plasticized PVC vinyl, and it's commonly used as a leather substitute in clothing or in a poll street.

15
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:38,320
 Because there are many different kinds of PVC with different amounts of plasticizer added for different particular applications, recycling over can be a bit tricky.

16
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:51,040
 It's difficult to know whether piece of PVC that you'd like to recycle exactly what the plasticizer content is, and mixing and matching a bunch of different PVCs with different plasticizer contents can result in one with a totally unknown plasticizer content,

17
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,840
 totally unpredictable material properties.

18
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:57,360
 For this reason, when we do recycle it, which isn't particularly often,

19
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:04,480
 Recycle PVC is typically used in lower quality applications in places where strength and stiffness isn't safety critical.

20
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:12,560
 When no one is at risk of dying, for example, because the plasticizer content and thus the material properties of the recycle product are particularly well known.
